<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Perimeter of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Take a right triangle with sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> Its perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} />, or <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ units}}]]]')} /> The number 12 tells you the complete distance around the triangular boundary: walk all three edges and you have travelled 12 units.</p>
  <p>This calculator does exactly that addition for any right triangle. You enter the two legs and the hypotenuse, and it returns the total boundary length with step-by-step work and a live diagram. Three side lengths are required because the boundary has three edges, and the hypotenuse must be included: it is one of those three edges even though it never touches the right angle. The two legs form the square corner, and the hypotenuse stretches across from it to close the shape.</p>
  <p>Perimeter is a linear measurement, not an area measurement. It answers "how much edging do I need?" rather than "how much surface is inside?" That distinction makes it the right number whenever you estimate fencing, trim, frames, wire, rope, or fabric borders around a right-triangular shape.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the geometry -->
<section id="sec-geometry" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Before any addition starts, the three sides must be labelled correctly. The symbols below stay fixed through the whole guide, and the calculator uses the same names.</p>

  <h3>Definition of a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>A right triangle is a triangle containing one interior angle measuring exactly 90 degrees. It has three vertices, three sides, two acute angles, and one right angle, usually marked with a small square. The two sides that form the right angle are the legs; the side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse. Because the angles of any triangle add to 180 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[A+B+C=180^\\circ]]')} />, and with <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A+B=90^\\circ}]]]')} />: the two acute angles are complementary, so knowing one determines the other. The angle sum matters when identifying a right triangle because it is the test the side lengths must pass: only a genuine 90-degree corner produces the side relationship the rest of this guide depends on.</p>

  <h3>What Are the Two Legs?</h3>
  <p>Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the two sides that meet at the right-angle vertex, perpendicular to each other. Either one can be drawn horizontal or vertical depending on the triangle's orientation; what defines them is the 90-degree corner they form, not their direction on the page. They differ from the hypotenuse in position and in length: each leg touches the right angle, and each leg is shorter than the hypotenuse. In perimeter work their role is simple: both are boundary edges, so both enter the sum.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, always the longest side of the triangle. It never forms the right angle; it spans across from it, joining the far ends of the two legs. It differs from the legs in both position and length: the legs meet at 90 degrees, while the hypotenuse meets each leg at an acute angle. It completes the triangular boundary, which is why it must be included in perimeter calculations. When both legs are known, the Pythagorean theorem recovers it: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>How to Identify the Correct Side Labels</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the two sides meeting at 90 degrees, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is opposite the right angle. In a non-degenerate right triangle <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the longest side. The right-angle marker tells you which side is <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />: it is the one that does not touch that vertex. A quick ordering check helps: sort the three numbers, and the largest must play the role of <span set:html={rich('[[c]].')} /> If the largest side does not sit opposite the square corner, the labels are wrong, and every calculation built on them will be wrong too.</p>

  <h3>Does the Perimeter Formula Work Only for Right Triangles?</h3>
  <p>There is an important mathematical distinction here. The formula <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} /> works for every triangle, right or not: any three-sided boundary adds up the same way. What the right-angle condition adds is the special relationship <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />, which is useful for finding missing sides and for checking whether the measurements describe a right triangle at all. The perimeter formula is universal; the Pythagorean relationship is the right triangle's bonus.</p>

