<!-- Section 3: Understanding the perimeter -->
<section id="sec-definition" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Perimeter of a Right Triangle?</h2>

  <h3>Definition of Perimeter</h3>
  <p>Perimeter is the total distance around the outer boundary of a two-dimensional shape. For a triangle it is the sum of its three side lengths: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=a+b+c}]]]')} /> All three sides must be included because the boundary is not complete until every edge has been counted. Leaving one out does not give a shorter perimeter; it gives a different, wrong quantity.</p>

  <h3>Why Does the Hypotenuse Count Toward the Perimeter?</h3>
  <p>Perimeter measures the complete outer boundary, and the hypotenuse is not an optional or internal measurement. It is one of the three outer edges. Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> The incorrect calculation <span set:html={rich('[[3+4=7]]')} /> counts only the two legs, the sides of the square corner. The correct calculation <span set:html={rich('[[3+4+5=12]]')} /> walks the full triangle and returns to the start. The value 7 is the combined length of the two legs, not the full perimeter, because the path from the end of leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> back to the start of leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> runs along the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>How Does the Perimeter Formula Work?</h3>
  <p>The formula breaks into three parts. First side: <span set:html={rich('[[a]].')} /> Second side: <span set:html={rich('[[b]].')} /> Third side: <span set:html={rich('[[c]].')} /> Combined: <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]].')} /> Addition is sufficient because perimeter is a length total, and lengths along one path simply add. No division is required, no squaring, no trigonometry: the formula is the simplest possible use of the boundary definition.</p>

  <h3>What Does Perimeter Represent in Real Life?</h3>
  <p>Perimeter is the total boundary length: the material required to go around an object once. That makes it the number behind edge measurements, frame dimensions, border lengths, fencing requirements, trim and molding, and rope and wire lengths. Measuring the outside boundary is a different job from measuring the surface enclosed inside the triangle. Fencing a triangular garden needs the perimeter; covering the same garden with turf needs the area. Confusing the two buys the wrong material.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Step-by-step method -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find the Perimeter of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The calculation follows a clear sequence. Each step has a purpose: identifying, checking, substituting, adding, and verifying. The 3-4-5 triangle demonstrates every step.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the three sides.</strong> Gather the measurements <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> This step collects the raw data before any arithmetic.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify the two perpendicular legs.</strong> Confirm that 3 and 4 are the legs, the sides forming the 90-degree corner. This assigns <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> their correct roles.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify the hypotenuse opposite the right angle.</strong> Confirm that 5 is the hypotenuse: it is the longest side and sits opposite the square corner. This prevents the most common labelling error.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 4: Confirm that all three measurements use compatible units.</strong> All three are plain units here, so no conversion is needed. This step guards against adding metres to feet.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5: Write the formula.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]].')} /> Writing it down keeps the structure visible while substituting.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 6: Substitute the known measurements.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]].')} /> Each symbol is replaced by its number.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 7: Add the three values.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]].')} /> One addition is the whole arithmetic.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 8: Attach the correct linear measurement unit.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ units}}]]]')} /> Perimeter is a length, so it takes linear units, never square units.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 9: Verify the result.</strong> Check <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]].')} /> The sides satisfy the Pythagorean theorem, so the triangle is genuinely right-angled and the perimeter is trustworthy.</p>

  <h3>Why Is It Important to Identify the Sides Correctly?</h3>
  <p>Incorrect labelling affects everything downstream. Mathematical verification fails when the hypotenuse is mislabelled, because the Pythagorean check expects <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> to be the longest side. Missing-side calculations use different formulas for a missing leg versus a missing hypotenuse, so a swapped label applies the wrong one. Calculator inputs are keyed to <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, so swapped values produce a plausible-looking but wrong answer. The live triangle visualization will also look distorted when a leg value sits in the hypotenuse slot. Getting the labels right once protects every later step.</p>

  <h3>Can I Calculate Perimeter Without Knowing the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>Yes, with one extra step first. If both legs are known, the hypotenuse can be found using the Pythagorean theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]].')} /> Once <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is known, the perimeter follows by ordinary addition. This does not imply that the existing perimeter calculator accepts only two sides: the calculator needs all three inputs, so the missing side must be computed by hand (or with the side calculator) before entering values.</p>
</section>

