<!-- Section 6: Missing side -->
<section id="sec-missing" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Perimeter of a Right Triangle With a Missing Side</h2>
  <p>The existing perimeter calculator requires all three side lengths. If one side is unknown, calculate it first with the Pythagorean theorem, then add. The three cases below cover every possibility.</p>

  <h3>Case 1: Finding the Missing Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>When both legs are known: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Example: <span set:html={rich('[[a=6,\\;b=8]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{36+64}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=10}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now calculate: <span set:html={rich('[[P=6+8+10]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=24\\text{ units}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Case 2: Finding a Missing Leg</h3>
  <p>When hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> are known: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=\\sqrt{c^2-a^2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Example: <span set:html={rich('[[c=13,\\;a=5]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{13^2-5^2}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{169-25}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=12}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now calculate: <span set:html={rich('[[P=5+12+13]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=30\\text{ units}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Case 3: Finding the Other Missing Leg</h3>
  <p>When hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are known: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=\\sqrt{c^2-b^2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Example: <span set:html={rich('[[c=17,\\;b=15]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{17^2-15^2}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{289-225}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=8}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now calculate: <span set:html={rich('[[P=8+15+17]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=40\\text{ units}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Why Must You Find the Missing Side First?</h3>
  <p>Perimeter represents the complete boundary. Ignoring an unknown side produces an incomplete result: two known sides give a partial path, not the perimeter. These missing-side calculations are supporting mathematical methods, not additional modes in the existing perimeter calculator. When you know only two sides, the <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Right Triangle Side Calculator</a> recovers the third, and then the perimeter calculator finishes the job.</p>

