<!-- Section 8: Mathematical properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Special Properties of the Perimeter of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Beyond simple addition, the perimeter of a right triangle obeys several sharp inequalities. They double as quick sanity checks on any computed result.</p>

  <h3>The Perimeter Is Greater Than Twice the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>For every valid triangle, the triangle inequality gives <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]].')} /> Add <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> to both sides: <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c>2c]].')} /> Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P>2c}]]]')} /> In plain language: the two legs together are always longer than the hypotenuse, so adding the hypotenuse to both sides keeps the inequality pointing the same way. For the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[2c=10]]')} />, and 12 is indeed greater than 10.</p>

  <h3>The Perimeter Is Less Than Three Times the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Since the hypotenuse is longer than either leg, <span set:html={rich('[[a<c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b<c]].')} /> Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[a+b<2c]].')} /> Adding <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P<3c}]]]')} /> This is a useful quick check: any claimed perimeter at or above three times the hypotenuse cannot belong to a valid right triangle. For the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[3c=15]]')} />, and 12 is comfortably below it.</p>

  <h3>Perimeter of a 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>In a 45-45-90 triangle both legs have the same length. Let each leg equal <span set:html={rich('[[k]].')} /> The hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c=k\\sqrt{2}]].')} /> Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[P=k+k+k\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, or <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=k(2+\\sqrt{2})}]]]')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[k=5]]')} />, the perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[5(2+\\sqrt{2})\\approx17.07]]')} />: two legs of 5 plus a hypotenuse of about 7.07.</p>

  <h3>Perimeter of a 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>If the shortest leg is <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, then the longer leg is <span set:html={rich('[[k\\sqrt{3}]]')} /> and the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[2k]].')} /> Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[P=k+k\\sqrt{3}+2k]]')} />, or <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=k(3+\\sqrt{3})}]]]')} /> With <span set:html={rich('[[k=4]]')} />, the sides are 4, about 6.93, and 8, giving a perimeter of about 18.93.</p>

  <h3>Which Right Triangle Has the Largest Perimeter for a Fixed Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>Intuitively, when the hypotenuse stays fixed, the perimeter grows as the two legs grow together, and it is largest when neither leg is starved: when both legs are equal. That is the 45-45-90 triangle. The precise statement uses the inequality <span set:html={rich('[[a+b\\leq\\sqrt{2(a^2+b^2)}]].')} /> Since <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />, it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P\\leq c(1+\\sqrt{2})}]]]')} />, with equality exactly when <span set:html={rich('[[a=b]].')} /> So among all right triangles sharing one hypotenuse, the isosceles right triangle wins the perimeter contest.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: Perimeter vs area -->
<section id="sec-vs-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Difference Between Perimeter and Area?</h2>
  <p>Perimeter and area both describe a triangle, but they answer different questions. This section draws the line carefully.</p>

  <h3>Perimeter Measures the Outer Boundary</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=a+b+c}]]]')} /> measures the total distance around the triangle. Its units are linear: metres, centimetres, millimetres, feet, inches. It tells you how much material traces the edge.</p>

  <h3>Area Measures the Enclosed Surface</h3>
  <p>For a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} /> The two perpendicular legs serve as base and height, so no hypotenuse appears in the formula. Area uses square units: square metres, square centimetres, square feet, square inches. It tells you how much surface lies inside the boundary.</p>

  <h3>Worked Comparison Using a 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ units}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The numbers 12 and 6 describe completely different properties of the same triangle: one is the walk around it, the other is the ground it covers.</p>

  <h3>Can Two Right Triangles Have the Same Perimeter but Different Areas?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Perimeter alone does not uniquely determine area; the shape and proportions of the triangle affect its enclosed area. A 3-4-5 triangle has perimeter 12 and area 6. A 45-45-90 triangle can also have perimeter 12: from <span set:html={rich('[[P=k(2+\\sqrt{2})=12]]')} />, the leg is <span set:html={rich('[[k=\\frac{12}{2+\\sqrt{2}}\\approx3.51]]')} />, and its area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{k^2}{2}\\approx6.18]]')} /> square units, slightly larger than 6. Same boundary length, different interior: the isosceles shape encloses a touch more.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('perimeter-vs-area.svg')} alt="3-4-5 triangle with highlighted boundary P equals 12 and shaded interior area K equals 6" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The highlighted edge is the perimeter; the shaded interior is the area. Two different measurements.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Semiperimeter relationship -->
<section id="sec-semiperimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Relationship Between Perimeter and Semiperimeter?</h2>
  <p>Semiperimeter is half the full perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{P}{2}}]]]')} /> For a right triangle that is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} /> It stays a supporting measurement on this page, not a second main subject.</p>

  <h3>Numerical Example</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;b=4,\\;c=5]].')} /> The perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]]')} />, so the semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12}{2}=6]].')} /> Both are linear measurements in the same units; one is simply half the other.</p>

  <h3>Why Is Semiperimeter Useful?</h3>
  <p>Semiperimeter is an important intermediate measurement in triangle geometry. It appears in Heron's formula for area, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}]]')} />, and in the inradius relationship <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} />, and it simplifies several advanced geometry proofs. Whenever a formula asks for <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, compute the perimeter first and halve it. For a dedicated tool, use the <a href="/right-triangle-semiperimeter-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Right Triangle Semiperimeter Calculator</a>.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Units -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Measurement Units in Right Triangle Perimeter Calculations</h2>
  <p>Perimeter works in any length unit: metres, centimetres, millimetres, kilometres, feet, inches, yards, or miles. The number changes with the unit, but the triangle does not.</p>

  <h3>Why Must All Three Side Measurements Use Compatible Units?</h3>
  <p>Perimeter is calculated through addition, and addition only makes sense between like quantities. Adding 3 feet to 48 inches without conversion mixes two scales and produces a meaningless total. Convert every side to one common unit first, then add.</p>

  <h3>Worked Unit Conversion Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=48\\text{ inches}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Convert inches to feet: <span set:html={rich('[[48\\text{ inches}=4\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Now all three sides are in feet, so <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ ft}}]]]')} /> The conversion must happen before the addition, not after.</p>

  <h3>How the Calculator's Unit Selectors Help</h3>
  <p>Each of the three inputs on this page carries its own unit selector. When you pick different units for different sides, the calculator converts every input into the common unit automatically before adding: it reads the unit chosen for the first side and scales the other two to match, using exact length ratios (1 inch = 0.0254 m, 1 foot = 0.3048 m, and so on). The result is reported in that common unit. You can still convert by hand as shown above, but the selectors remove the arithmetic.</p>

  <h3>Working With Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a=2.5,\\;b=6,\\;c=6.5]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[P=2.5+6+6.5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=15\\text{ units}}]]]')} /> Decimal values are added in exactly the same way as whole numbers; the decimal points simply line up in the sum.</p>

  <h3>Working With Irrational Side Lengths</h3>
  <p>Take a triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a=1,\\;b=1,\\;c=\\sqrt{2}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[P=1+1+\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=2+\\sqrt{2}}]]]')} />, approximately <span set:html={rich('[[P\\approx3.4142]].')} /> Keeping the exact radical form is useful in mathematical problems because it preserves full precision; the decimal approximation is for final reporting.</p>

  <h3>Why Should You Avoid Rounding Too Early?</h3>
  <p>Premature rounding injects small errors that the final addition then keeps. If a side is irrational, like <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, rounding it to 1.41 before adding shaves off part of the true length, and with several such sides the error accumulates. Keep full precision during intermediate calculations and round only the final result to the accuracy the problem requires.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Perimeter</h2>
  <p>Sixteen mistakes account for nearly every wrong perimeter. Each one is explained with its cause and the correct approach.</p>
  <p><strong>1. Forgetting to include the hypotenuse.</strong> The most common error: adding the two legs and stopping. With 3-4-5 this gives 7 instead of 12. Always add all three sides.</p>
  <p><strong>2. Using only the two legs.</strong> Same root cause as above, often from misreading the problem. The boundary has three edges; count three.</p>
  <p><strong>3. Confusing perimeter with area.</strong> Perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c]]')} /> in linear units; area is <span set:html={rich('[[ab/2]]')} /> in square units. Read the question: "around" means perimeter, "inside" means area.</p>
  <p><strong>4. Entering the hypotenuse as a leg.</strong> Putting the longest value in a leg field breaks the geometry. The hypotenuse slot must hold the longest side.</p>
  <p><strong>5. Selecting the wrong side as the hypotenuse.</strong> If 4 is entered as <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> while 5 sits in a leg field, every check fails. Sort the values first: the largest is <span set:html={rich('[[c]].')} /></p>
  <p><strong>6. Using measurements that do not form a right triangle.</strong> Test <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> before trusting the label "right triangle". If it fails, the perimeter formula still adds, but the triangle is not right-angled.</p>
  <p><strong>7. Mixing centimetres and metres.</strong> Adding 3 m to 40 cm gives neither 43 of anything. Convert to one unit first.</p>
  <p><strong>8. Mixing feet and inches.</strong> The classic imperial trap. Convert inches to feet (divide by 12) before adding, or let the unit selectors do it.</p>
  <p><strong>9. Reporting perimeter in square units.</strong> A perimeter of 12 cm squared is a category error: the walk around a triangle is a length, measured in cm, m, ft, or in.</p>
  <p><strong>10. Rounding measurements too early.</strong> Rounding <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> to 1.4 before adding accumulates error. Round the final answer only.</p>
  <p><strong>11. Forgetting to calculate a missing side.</strong> Two known sides are not a perimeter. Recover the third with the Pythagorean theorem first, then add.</p>
  <p><strong>12. Using the Pythagorean theorem incorrectly.</strong> The hypotenuse form adds squares (<span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} />); the leg form subtracts (<span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{c^2-a^2}]]')} />). Swapping them invents a side that does not exist.</p>
  <p><strong>13. Entering negative or zero side lengths.</strong> Lengths are positive. A zero or negative input describes no triangle, and the calculator rightly refuses it.</p>
  <p><strong>14. Ignoring the triangle inequality.</strong> If <span set:html={rich('[[a+b\\leq c]]')} />, the three lengths cannot form a triangle at all. Check before calculating.</p>
  <p><strong>15. Assuming semiperimeter is the full perimeter.</strong> Dividing the sum by 2 gives <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[P]].')} /> For 3-4-5, <span set:html={rich('[[(3+4+5)/2=6]]')} /> is the semiperimeter; the perimeter is 12.</p>
  <p><strong>16. Assuming the perimeter alone determines the triangle's area.</strong> As the 3-4-5 versus 45-45-90 comparison showed, equal perimeters can hide different areas. Perimeter fixes the boundary, not the interior.</p>
  <p><strong>Wrong versus correct, side by side.</strong> Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;b=4,\\;c=5]]')} />: incorrect is <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4=7]]')} />, which omits the hypotenuse and measures only the two legs. Correct is <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5=12]].')} /> And <span set:html={rich('[[(3+4+5)/2=6]]')} /> is the semiperimeter, a different quantity with its own uses, not the full perimeter.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Right Triangle Perimeter Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Seven independent checks catch almost every error. The 3-4-5 triangle demonstrates each one. Independent checks matter because they fail in different ways: when two unrelated methods agree, the answer has earned its trust, whether it goes into homework, technical drawings, or material planning.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 1: Add all three sides again.</strong> Recompute <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} /> from scratch: <span set:html={rich('[[3+4+5=12]].')} /> A second pass catches keystroke and arithmetic slips.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 2: Verify the Pythagorean relationship.</strong> Check <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]].')} /> This confirms the sides form a right triangle, which validates the side labels the perimeter depends on.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 3: Check the hypotenuse.</strong> Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[c>a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c>b]]')} />: 5 is greater than 3 and greater than 4. If the supposed hypotenuse is not the longest side, the labels are wrong.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 4: Check the triangle inequality.</strong> Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[3+4=7>5]].')} /> The sum of any two sides must exceed the third, or no triangle exists.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 5: Check the perimeter bounds.</strong> Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[2c<P<3c]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[10<12<15]].')} /> These bounds are quick mental checks for any valid right triangle.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 6: Check the measurement units.</strong> Confirm all three inputs share one unit. Mixed units invalidate the sum even when the arithmetic is perfect.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 7: Compare the result with the live visualization.</strong> The existing diagram on this page redraws the triangle from your inputs. If a side was mislabelled or a value mistyped, the picture looks wrong: a hypotenuse shorter than a leg, or a shape that does not close. Trust the picture when it disagrees with the number, then find the input error.</p>
