  <h2>Three Sides, One Boundary, No Shortcuts</h2>
  <p>The perimeter of a right triangle is the simplest idea on this page and the easiest to get wrong by omission. The formula asks for three numbers because the boundary has three edges, and the hypotenuse earns its place in the sum as the edge that closes the shape. Everything else, the step-by-step method, the missing-side recovery, the inequalities, the verification checks, exists to protect that one addition from the small labelling and unit errors that produce plausible-looking wrong answers.</p>
  <p>Use this page's calculator whenever all three sides are measured, recover a missing side through the Pythagorean theorem when they are not, and check the result against the bounds 2c &lt; P &lt; 3c before the number goes into a material order. When the question turns to the surface inside the boundary, the area calculator is waiting; when it turns to half the boundary for a deeper formula, the semiperimeter calculator takes over.</p>
</section>
